{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The checklist is explicitly authored and frozen, whereas the expert's theory is implicit and mutable.","The panel provides redundancy that can catch execution errors, while a single expert has no such redundancy."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is shown to be a surface heuristic, not a causal model.","The expert's theory is shown to be causally superior to the checklist."],"shared_structure":["Both domains involve executing a theory (frozen checklist vs internal intuition) against ground truth.","Reliability depends on theory-quality × execution-fidelity, not panel-vs-individual."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["Adversarial items that break the observed→latent edge will distinguish causal structure from surface proxy."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel using an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single intuition-reliant expert ONLY when the checklist encodes a theory whose causal structure is superior to the expert's working theory. The panel's value is not aggregation (Condorcet) but execution fidelity to a superior frozen theory.","counterconditions":["The checklist is merely a surface heuristic that tracks the right answer on the evaluated distribution without capturing causal structure.","The expert's internal theory is causally superior to the checklist's frozen theory, as in a novel domain the checklist has not caught up to.","The panel fails to execute the checklist faithfully, introducing noise that overwhelms the fidelity benefit."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklist items are observable proxies for latent causes. The panel faithfully executes the checklist author's theory. The expert executes their own internal theory, which is subject to cognitive bias and mutable heuristics. The comparison is therefore 'frozen-good-theory executed faithfully' vs 'mutable-theory executed with bias'. If the frozen theory captures true causal structure, the panel wins; if the expert's internal theory is superior (e.g., in a novel domain), the expert wins.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["This proposal frames the distinction as additive vs configural processing, not frozen-theory vs mutable-theory.","This proposal does not require adversarial items, only items with configural structure."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist explicitly encodes configural rules, so compliance produces configural correctness.","The domain lacks configural structure."],"shared_structure":["Both use item-type performance to distinguish deep understanding from surface compliance.","Both predict that configural items are the critical test case."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["If the panel succeeds on configural items, the checklist has transmitted genuine reasoning.","If the panel fails configural items, it is merely compliant."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single-evaluation discriminator is the panel's differential performance on configural items (where cues interact non-additively) vs additive items. Genuine reliability gain manifests as correct handling of configural structure; mere checklist compliance manifests as getting additive items right while failing configural items.","counterconditions":["The domain has no configural structure (all items are additive), making the discriminator moot.","The checklist explicitly instructs on configural interactions, so compliance IS configural processing.","The expert also fails configural items, so differential performance is uninformative."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist that merely lists cues invites additive processing (sum the cues). Genuine reliability requires configural processing (weight cues by their interaction). If the panel outperforms the expert on configural items, the checklist has transmitted genuine causal structure. If the panel matches the expert on additive items but fails configural items, the panel is merely compliant with a surface heuristic.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","d3fb85801340797b053ab06f7e489ce2d71194d458dc9d3951d843439ed58209"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["This proposal is more skeptical, claiming necessity of cross-distribution evidence.","SRC_005 treats the problem's phrase 'within a single evaluation' as justification for total abstention."],"overturn_conditions":["Adversarial items within a single evaluation are shown to break surface correlations.","The checklist is provably causally complete."],"shared_structure":["Both question whether a single evaluation can distinguish deep structure from surface tracking.","Both point to cross-distribution evidence as necessary for definitive distinction."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["If the distinction cannot be made within a single evaluation, the problem's request is unfulfillable.","Cross-distribution testing is the covering principle for this distinction."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, one cannot definitively distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance that tracks the right answer, because a single evaluation is one sample from a possibly narrow distribution. The distinction requires cross-distribution evidence by necessity.","counterconditions":["The evaluation includes adversarial items specifically designed to break surface correlations, which could suffice within one evaluation.","The checklist is provably causally complete (deductive), collapsing the distinction.","The evaluation distribution is known to be representative and broad, reducing cross-distribution concerns."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A single evaluation, even with adversarial items, cannot rule out that the checklist is a surface proxy that happens to be causally complete on the evaluated distribution. To distinguish genuine gain from compliance-tracking, one must test whether the reliability holds across distributions where the surface correlations might break. This is a structural limitation of single-evaluation evidence.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","26852eb420d816389e3bab11a8cb7451a199bf45e38828f38e0bfe69efe0d84e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["This proposal claims the distinction collapses if the checklist is causally complete.","SRC_002 treats causal completeness vs surface heuristics as the distinction itself, not as a collapse condition."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is shown to be causally complete.","The expert's theory is shown to be causally complete while the checklist is not."],"shared_structure":["Both hinge on whether the checklist captures causal structure.","Both predict that the distinction depends on causal completeness."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["If the checklist is causally complete, the panel always wins and compliance is genuine.","If not, cross-distribution evidence is needed."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if the checklist is causally complete (i.e., deductive). If the checklist is causally complete, the panel always ≥ expert and the compliance-vs-genuine distinction collapses because compliance IS the mechanism. If the checklist is not causally complete, the distinction is critical but may be unresolvable within a single evaluation.","counterconditions":["The checklist is causally incomplete and the evaluation distribution is narrow.","The expert's theory is causally complete while the checklist is not.","Causal completeness is unprovable within the evaluation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal completeness means the checklist's items fully determine the outcome. If so, faithful execution guarantees correctness, and compliance is genuine reliability. The panel's value is execution fidelity. If the checklist is not causally complete, it is a heuristic, and compliance may or may not track the right answer. The distinction then depends on whether the heuristic's correlations hold, which is a cross-distribution question.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["This proposal focuses on error distribution shape, not item-type performance.","This proposal does not require adversarial items, only sufficient errors to analyze patterns."],"overturn_conditions":["The expert and checklist author share the same theory.","There are too few errors to analyze patterns."],"shared_structure":["Both use error patterns to distinguish mechanisms.","Both predict that different mechanisms produce different error signatures."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["If the panel's errors are uncorrelated with the expert's, the panel is using a different (checklist) theory.","If the panel's errors cluster where surface proxies fail, the panel is merely compliant."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single-evaluation discriminator is the SHAPE of the panel's error distribution relative to the expert's, not just mean accuracy. Genuine reliability gain manifests as reduced variance and correlated errors with the checklist author's theory; mere compliance manifests as the same mean accuracy but with errors clustered on items where the checklist's surface proxies fail.","counterconditions":["The expert and the checklist author share the same theory, so error patterns are identical regardless of mechanism.","The evaluation is too small to detect error pattern differences.","The checklist's surface proxies never diverge from the outcome on the evaluated distribution."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the panel is genuinely reasoning via the checklist's causal structure, its errors should be uncorrelated with the expert's intuition-based errors and instead reflect the checklist theory's blind spots. If the panel is merely compliant with a surface heuristic, its errors will cluster where the surface proxy diverges from the outcome. The error pattern, not the accuracy, is the signature.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","d3fb85801340797b053ab06f7e489ce2d71194d458dc9d3951d843439ed58209"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["This proposal claims the distinction is a category error for heuristic checklists.","SRC_008 reduces the distinction to causal completeness vs surface heuristics but does not claim category error."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is claimed to be causal.","The problem implicitly assumes the checklist is causal."],"shared_structure":["Both question whether the distinction is meaningful for non-causal checklists.","Both predict that the distinction depends on the checklist's claimed status."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["For heuristic checklists, compliance is the only mechanism and the distinction is moot.","For causal checklists, compliance with causal structure is genuine reasoning."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between genuine reliability gain and mere compliance is a category error when the checklist is explicitly a surface heuristic. For such checklists, compliance IS the mechanism, and the distinction is moot. The problem only makes sense if the checklist is claimed to be causal.","counterconditions":["The checklist is claimed to be causal, so the distinction is meaningful.","The expert uses the checklist as a heuristic but adds intuition, creating a hybrid case.","The problem implicitly assumes the checklist is causal."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the checklist is explicitly a surface heuristic (e.g., 'check these boxes because they correlate with the outcome'), then there is no 'genuine reasoning' to contrast with compliance. The panel's reliability is entirely due to compliance with the heuristic. The distinction only arises if the checklist is claimed to capture causal structure, in which case compliance with the causal structure IS genuine reasoning.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","a3c76d8b070618619dcd11038696e0aff7a612fb29919846dee09789a4c974e8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}